From f06a2e6fc8827c4aa84cc2ceebe46af950c74af4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrin Jalali <adrin.jalali@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 15:58:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [3.14] gh-152235: Put objects a collection revives back in
 address order

When a full collection reaches an object before its only referrer, it
moves the object to 'unreachable', and visit_reachable() moves it back
to the end of 'young' when the referrer turns out to be reachable, in
the referrer's traversal order.  For a set that is hash order: its
elements end up in random memory order in the GC list, and every later
full collection is up to ten times slower.  This happens to a set built
from a generator (since gh-152235 only tracks it once it is full) and to
one built from a list or dict that then goes away (in every version).

Sort each batch of revived objects by address instead.  They are still
behind their referrer, so later collections don't move them again.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
